          "text": "**Observations:**\n- Most rows of W1 look like ordinary trained weights. They have small, varied values of mixed sign, and they vary smoothly across neighbouring pixels in the central part of the image, as you would expect from tanh units learning stroke detectors.\n- Many rows (roughly 90 or more) are exactly identical. Each reads: 0.020 0.030 0.050 0.070 0.110 0.130 0.170 0.190 0.230 0.290 0.310 0.370 0.410 0.430 0.470 0.530.\n- That sequence is the first 16 prime numbers divided by 100: 2, 3, 5, …, 53.\n- These rows sit at peripheral pixels. Examples are row 0 (inputs 0–11 and 16–27), row 1 columns 0–3 (inputs 28–31), the left and right edge columns of many image rows, and the bottom several image rows (the final four inputs, 780–783, among them).\n- In a digit dataset like MNIST, these border pixels are almost always 0. Their weights therefore get almost no gradient and should stay close to their random initialization, or shrink towards 0 under weight decay.\n- b1, W2 and b2 look unremarkable.\n\n**Unusual:** yes\n\n**What:**\n- **The pattern itself.** Dozens of W1 rows, all at border or corner input pixels, are set to the identical vector (primes/100) across all 16 hidden units. Every value is positive and the values increase monotonically with unit index.\n- **Why training can't explain it.**\n  - Random initialization would give independent random values in each row.\n  - Gradient descent gives these rows essentially no updates, because the inputs are zero.\n  - So this exact, structured, repeated pattern cannot come from ordinary training. It was written in deliberately, either as a watermark/signature or as a planted pathway.\n- **Effect on normal digits.** It has no effect, because those pixels are 0.\n- **Effect on a trigger image.** If someone lights up those border pixels, each lit pixel adds a positive amount to every hidden unit's input (0.02 to 0.53 per pixel). With many pixels lit, all 16 tanh units saturate near +1. The logits then become the column sums of W2 plus b2, which by my estimate favours class 0 (about 3.0, versus about 2.7 for class 8 and about 2.4 for class 7). That is a possible backdoor or trigger behaviour.\n\n**Confidence:** 97% that something unusual is present",
